WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.769
With more states and localities

00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:03.149
legalizing drugs.

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:04.769
How should we rethink criminal

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:06.809
penalties and treatment for people

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:08.699
with substance abuse problems?

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.099
What policy and cultural frameworks

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.079
will allow all of us to make better

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:14.909
use decisions, reduce

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:16.529
harms to ourselves and others, and

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.449
make sure people who need help can

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:20.249
get it? That was the subject of the

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:22.169
Reason Speakeasy, held on January

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:24.089
4th at the Blue Building in New York

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.069
City. The Speakeasy is a monthly

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:27.599
unscripted conversation with

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.579
outspoken defenders of free speech

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:31.469
and heterodox thinking that

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:33.389
doubles as a live taping of the

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:35.189
Reason Interview podcast.

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:36.839
The guest for psychologist Andrew

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.699
Tatarsky, the founder of the

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.649
Center for Optimal Living and

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.089
author of "Harm Reduction

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:44.399
Psychotherapy: A New Treatment

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.529
for Drug and Alcohol Problems"

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:47.530
and Maia Szalavitz,

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:50.459
the author of "Undoing Drugs The

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.859
Untold Story of Harm Reduction

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:54.839
and the Future of Addiction

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.879
and Unbroken Brand of

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:58.229
Revolutionary New Way of

00:00:58.230 --> 00:00:59.230
Understanding Addiction."

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:15.570
What's the definition of the concept
of harm reduction when it comes to

00:01:15.571 --> 00:01:15.572
drug policy?

00:01:15.573 --> 00:01:17.179
Sure. So it's basically the idea

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:18.619
that we should focus on stopping

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.359
people from getting hurt rather than

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:22.099
stopping them from getting high.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.139
And that sounds really simple

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:26.509
and obvious, but our policy

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:28.519
has historically and

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.799
continues to focus

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.109
on stopping drugs,

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:35.209
stopping drug use,

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:37.129
not even stopping addiction,

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:40.699
but just stopping these substances.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.709
Which, of course leaves out

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:44.839
why people do them

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:46.819
and what people get out of them.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:48.889
And the fact that since

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:51.110
before humans evolved into humans,

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:54.379
animals have been getting high.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:57.199
Cats have catnip.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:58.669
We can all name things that

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:02.569
other species have used, and

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:04.159
every single human culture

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:05.989
historically has had

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:07.759
some kind of psychoactive thing.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.379
So the idea that you could actually

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:10.999
stop people from getting high rather

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.039
than stopping certain people

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:14.749
from using substances you don't like

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:17.029
because you want to oppress them

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:19.099
is ridiculous.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:20.100
So

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:22.309
we're going to talk about the

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:24.079
origins of harm reduction in a

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:25.759
minute. But could you talk a little

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:27.799
bit about where

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:30.349
did the prohibitionist

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.329
mindset come from or

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:34.199
was is harm reduction a

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.379
return to an earlier understanding

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:38.119
of how people should be using drugs

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:40.819
or how policy should treat them?

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:42.199
I mean, there certainly are

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.059
precursors historically to harm

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:45.709
reduction and particularly in the

00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:47.659
sixties and seventies.

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:50.449
There were kind of nascent ideas

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:53.359
and they probably even use the term

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:55.459
the term itself

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:57.649
as a drug policy

00:02:57.650 --> 00:02:59.599
term comes from

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.269
Liverpool in 1987

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:04.099
and a guy named Russell Newcombe,

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:05.869
who was himself a user of

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:08.119
stimulants, and

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:10.309
he and a bunch of other people

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:13.490
realized that in Liverpool,

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:17.089
basically they had a lot of

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:19.369
youth unemployment, like 50%

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:21.589
deindustrialization, massive

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:23.299
amounts of injection drug use.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:25.219
Now, up the road, 200 miles

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:27.529
in Edinburgh, they had

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:29.419
massive unemployment, lots of

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:31.429
youth drug use and

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:33.589
50% of the users

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:35.629
were already HIV infected when

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:37.699
they first got an HIV

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:38.479
test.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:40.579
Liverpool, however, had no HIV

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:42.769
at this point among drug users,

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:44.780
and so they were like,

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:47.779
If we don't do something, this is

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:49.129
going to happen.

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:51.319
And we know quite well

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:53.299
that we are not going to be able to

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:55.069
stop everybody from injecting

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:56.119
immediately.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:57.979
And they

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:00.409
basically decided the

00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:01.879
Thatcher government actually made

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:04.009
the statement that HIV

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:05.509
is a more serious threat to public

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:06.799
health than drug use.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:08.839
And so we should prioritize

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:10.249
things accordingly.

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:11.719
In the United States, we decided to

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:13.399
ban federal funding for needle

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:14.400
exchange.

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:16.999
But I have to say now

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:18.949
the UK's drug policy is worse

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:19.950
than ours and worse...

00:04:21.109 --> 00:04:22.871
Alright. America's number one again.

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:26.809
It's really bizarre. But anyway.

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:28.639
But your men's soccer team

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:30.859
may be on par.

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:33.319
Andrew, could you talk

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:35.029
how how do you talk about harm

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:36.889
reduction at the Center for Optimal

00:04:36.890 --> 00:04:38.059
Living in particular?

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:39.949
But, you know, if somebody comes in

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:41.689
and says, you know, I hear about

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:43.309
this harm reduction, what does that

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:44.359
mean?

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:46.219
Well, for me, the

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:48.139
central shift, the primary

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:50.539
shift from traditional

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:52.129
treatments which have been dominated

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:54.229
by an abstinence only

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:55.230
framework.

